<section class="eg-section eg-section-tint" id="faq">
  <div class="eg-container eg-narrow">
    <div class="eg-section-head"><span class="eg-eyebrow">Before your first workflow</span><h2>Practical answers for developers.</h2></div>
    <div class="eg-faq">
      <details><summary>How does EZGraph relate to LangGraph and LangChain?</summary><p>EZGraph compiles its authoring layer to a LangGraph <code>StateGraph</code>. LangGraph executes it; LangChain supplies underlying components. EZGraph adds a shared application contract for nodes, tools, histories, state, models, recovery, and sessions. LangChain also supplies agent loops and middleware; <a href="/ezgraph/compare/langgraph/">compare the conventions</a> to decide which layer fits.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I start without an account or model credentials?</summary><p>Yes. EZGraph needs no account or license key. The downloadable starter uses scripted model replies and local memory sessions. Live models require your chosen provider's credentials, or a custom adapter for your own endpoint. Model-provider and infrastructure costs are separate.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What does the runtime license allow?</summary><p>Production application use has no EZGraph runtime fee, including commercial applications and internal use. The license restricts competing hosted services and redistribution as a competing SDK, developer library, or plugin framework. Read the <a href="/ezgraph/license/">full license</a> for the governing terms.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is the runtime open source?</summary><p>The public npm runtime is distributed as compiled, obfuscated JavaScript with TypeScript declarations. The example application source is public. Optional paid support includes access to private readable source and a debug package while the subscription is active. <a href="/contact/">Contact PicoFlow for support details.</a></p></details>
      <details><summary>How should I adopt it in an existing LangGraph application?</summary><p>Start with a new workflow or a small stage. Adapt its prompt, tools, state, and transitions to the node contract, then test the behavior through the turn harness. Existing applications need deliberate adaptation to EZGraph's state and session contracts. The <a href="/ezgraph/why/#adoption">adoption guide</a> maps those responsibilities.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does tool scoping make model behavior fully reliable?</summary><p>Tool scoping narrows the actions available at each stage. Schema checks, validation, explicit transitions, and recovery hooks give your application more control. Your handlers still own authorization, domain validation, and side effects; models still need evaluation on real scenarios.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
